<p>The U.S. Post Office Department issued the four-cent Lincoln coil stamp and its associated first-day covers at the Mandan post office on July 31, 1958. The stamp features President {% person "abraham-lincoln" %}Abraham Lincoln{% endperson %} and was issued to accommodate the increase in the first-class postage rate from three to four cents on August 1, 1958—the first increase in 26 years. Originally issued on November 16, 1954, the red-violet design features Lincoln's likeness based on a portrait by {% person "douglas-volk" %}Douglas Volk{% endperson %}. First-class postage remained four cents through January 6, 1963, and the stamp was widely used during that period. It replaced the three-cent purple "Statue of Liberty—In God We Trust" stamp.</p>

<p>A first-day cover (FDC) is an envelope bearing a newly issued stamp, a cancellation from its first day of issue, and often unique artwork known as a cachet. The FDCs for rolls of the coil stamp were issued at the Mandan post office, selected because of its proximity to Fort Abraham Lincoln. Covers for stamps issued in booklet panes were released the same day in Wheeling, West Virginia.</p>

<p>Abraham Lincoln currently ranks third among subjects depicted on U.S. postage stamps. Only {% person "george-washington" %}George Washington{% endperson %} and {% person "thomas-jefferson" %}Thomas Jefferson{% endperson %} have appeared on more issues.</p>

<p>First-day covers are collected nationally. At least 20 cacheted covers and one blank envelope were canceled in Mandan that day. Examples appear above.</p>

<p>{% person "margaret-bowers-bingenheimer" %}Mrs. Margaret (George H.) Bingenheimer{% endperson %}, {% person "fanny-bowers" %}Mrs. Fanny (John L.) Bowers{% endperson %}, and {% person "anne-cary" %}Mrs. Anne (Lyman N.) Cary{% endperson %}, all early Mandan pioneers and wives of prominent early businessmen, were given the honor of purchasing the first new four-cent Lincoln coil stamps.</p>

<p>In the weeks before the release, the Mandan Post Office received more than 100,000 letters for first-day cancellations. According to Mandan postmaster {% person "j-j-murray" %}J. J. Murray{% endperson %}, more than {% inflation 60000, 1958 %} worth of the new 500-stamp rolls were on hand for the first day of issue. In addition to the 100,000 letters, 50,000 envelopes were canceled for stamp dealers across the country and around the world. First-day cachets were affixed to envelopes from France, Australia, and the Philippines. The permanent staff was supplemented by 18 temporary workers to assist with the first-day issues.</p>
